WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.198 --> 00:00:05.002
And that brings this class on LangChain,
Chat with Your Data, to an end.

2
00:00:05.903 --> 00:00:09.893
In this class, we've covered how to use
LangChain to load data

3
00:00:09.893 --> 00:00:12.886
from a variety of
document sources using LangChain's 80-plus

4
00:00:12.886 --> 00:00:13.883
different document
loaders.

5
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.102
From there,
we split the documents into chunks,

6
00:00:17.102 --> 00:00:20.682
and talk about a lot of the nuances
that arrive when doing so.

7
00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:24.799
After that, we take those chunks,
create embeddings for them,

8
00:00:24.799 --> 00:00:27.660
and put them into a vector store,
showing how that

9
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.804
easily enables semantic search.

10
00:00:29.523 --> 00:00:32.615
But we also talk about some of the
downsides of semantic search,

11
00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:35.707
and where it can fail in
the certain edge cases that arise.

12
00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:39.420
The next thing that
we cover is retrieval,

13
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.576
maybe my favorite part of the class,
where we talk about a lot of new

14
00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.793
and advanced and really
fun retrieval algorithms for

15
00:00:45.793 --> 00:00:46.901
overcoming those edge cases.

16
00:00:48.382 --> 00:00:50.857
We combine that with
LLMs in the next session,

17
00:00:50.857 --> 00:00:52.508
where we take
those retrieved documents,

18
00:00:52.508 --> 00:00:55.533
we take the user question,
we pass it to an LLM,

19
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:58.008
and we generate an
answer to the original question.

20
00:00:58.008 --> 00:00:59.933
But there's
still one thing missing, which

21
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:01.583
is the conversational
aspect of it.

22
00:01:02.221 --> 00:01:04.814
And that's
where we finish out the class, by

23
00:01:04.814 --> 00:01:07.406
creating a fully functioning end-to-end chatbot
over your data.

24
00:01:08.959 --> 00:01:10.580
I've
really enjoyed teaching this class. I

25
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.202
hope you guys have enjoyed taking it.

26
00:01:12.878 --> 00:01:15.599
And I want to thank everyone in the
open source who has

27
00:01:15.599 --> 00:01:17.413
contributed a lot of
the things that make

28
00:01:17.413 --> 00:01:19.908
this class possible,
like all the prompts and a lot of

29
00:01:19.908 --> 00:01:21.041
the functionality that you see.

30
00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:24.797
As you guys build
with LangChain and discover

31
00:01:24.797 --> 00:01:27.439
new ways of doing
things and new tips and techniques,

32
00:01:28.057 --> 00:01:31.765
I hope that you share what you'll
learn on Twitter or even open

33
00:01:31.765 --> 00:01:35.473
up a PR in LangChain. It's a
really fast-moving field, and it's an

34
00:01:35.473 --> 00:01:36.043
exciting time.

35
00:01:36.602 --> 00:01:38.284
I really look forward
to seeing how you

36
00:01:38.284 --> 00:01:39.966
apply everything that you
learned in this class.
